<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.15.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00337</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.15.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>2a915bb9-a73b-498e-ab78-d42012ecdef9</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="practice">
      <para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
      <exercise id="_87ea4671-e924-4106-8958-0fb20fd70e4b" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>If y varies directly as \(x\) and \(y=14\), when \(x=3\). Find the equation that relates \(x\) and \(y\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! \(y=\frac{14}3x\)</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.15.1.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(y=\frac{14}3x\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="e801f482-f79e-445c-a137-4b9b16a764c9" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>The price, \(P\), that Eric pays for gas varies directly with the number of gallons, \(g\), he buys. It costs him $50 to buy 20 gallons of gas. Choose the equation that relates \(P\) and \(g\).</para>
          <para>Multiple Choice:</para>
          <para>\(P = 25g\)</para>
          <para>\(P=\frac{2.5}g\)</para>
          <para>\(G=\frac{2.5}p\)</para>
          <para>\(P=\frac{2.5}g\)</para>
          <para>\(P=\frac g{2.5}\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! \(P = 2.5g\)</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is \(P = 2.5g\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="db54efcd-a1a1-4135-83cc-fd19b4ae9897" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>Joseph is traveling on a road trip. The distance, \(d\), he travels before stopping for lunch varies directly with the speed, \(v\), he travels. He can travel 120 miles at a speed of 60 mph. How many miles would he travel before stopping for lunch at a rate of 65 mph?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! 130 miles</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 130 miles.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="c88ca437-735d-4652-8b7a-c31bdc723a57" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>The mass of a liquid, \(M\), varies directly with its volume, \(V\). A liquid with mass 16 kilograms has a volume of 2 liters. What is the volume, in liters, of this liquid if its mass is 128 kilograms?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! 16 liters. \(M=8V\)</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 16 liters. \(M=8V\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_968a0e07-07a0-4dfb-ab3f-a99e74be221f" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>The length, \(L\), that a spring stretches varies directly with a weight, \(W\), placed at the end of the spring. When Sarah placed a 10-pound watermelon on a hanging scale, the spring stretched 5 inches. What weight of watermelon, in pounds, would stretch the spring 6 inches?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! 12 pounds, \(L=1/2 W\).</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 12 pounds, \(L=\frac12W\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="f84ffff3-68b7-4dea-9903-7a1ca6ab2545" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>The maximum load a beam, \( M\), will support varies directly with the square of the diagonal of the beam’s cross-section, \(d^2\). A beam with diagonal 6 inch will support a maximum load of 108 pounds. What load, in pounds, will a beam with a 10-inch diagonal support?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! 300 pounds. Use the direct variation equation, \(M=3d^2\).</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is 300 pounds. Use the direct variation equation, \(M=3d^2\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="_0ff5da75-2446-49a8-bd92-332f581059a2" class="multiple-choice">
        <problem>
          <para>The number of calories, \(c\), burned varies directly with the amount of time, \(t\), spent exercising. Arnold burned 312 calories in 65 minutes exercising. How many calories would he burn if he exercises for 90 minutes?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Correct! He would burn 432 calories.</para>
          <para>Try again. Revisit activities 1.15.2 and 1.15.3.</para>
          <para>The correct answer is is a. \(M\)\(=8V\); b. 16 liters.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
